/* V84 — harder, by arriving faster rather than by hitting harder.

   ---- What "harder" has to mean here ----

   The arena is empty 53-82% of the time with a median of ZERO hostiles on screen, and the spawner
   is why: `02.part` waits `max(.22, 1.05 - wave*.035)` seconds between arrivals — a fraction over a
   second at wave one — and V56 holds a floor of one to three on screen. A player who can remove an
   arrival in 0.2 seconds is never behind, and nothing else in the game can make them behind.

   That is also why V83's magazine changed nothing measurable on its own: a tap budget cannot bind
   on a player who has nothing to tap. Measured with six shots and a 1.8/second reload, the bot sat
   at a full magazine for an entire run.

   So the difficulty is the arrival rate, and it always was. Everything else — stronger hostiles,
   more hit points, faster fall — makes a hostile cost two free taps instead of one.

   ---- Why more arrivals is not the thing that was complained about ----

   The owner's complaint was "too many monsters, which makes the game uninteresting", and V56 was
   cut hard in response. That complaint was right about the symptom and the cut was right at the
   time, because a crowd with no answer is noise: the only tool the player had was to tap each
   member, so a crowd was six times the work and none of the decisions.

   Everything since has been building the answer. V63 made where you break a group decide how much
   of it goes; V64 arranged them so the break point matters; V76 lets the player gather a crowd
   where they chose it; V81 made the six archetypes readable at a glance instead of six coloured
   circles; V82 made a wave worth protecting; V83 makes a cascade the affordable way to clear one.

   A crowd is now a harvest rather than a chore, which is what makes this raise a different change
   from the one that was cut. Pressure is a small number of legible threats arriving faster than
   comfortable; noise is a large number you cannot tell apart. This raises the rate and leaves the
   simultaneous count near the tracking limit, deliberately.

   ---- Applied as a drain, not a rewrite ----

   The spawn interval is reset inside `02.part`'s update, in the middle of the loop, with no seam.
   Rather than reimplementing that line here — which would put two copies of the arrival curve in
   the tree and guarantee they drift — the timer is drained faster, which is exactly how V56 and V21
   both already bend it. Draining at 1.6x is a 1.6x arrival rate, arrived at by the one mechanism the
   rest of the codebase already uses. */

const V84={
 on:true,
 /* Extra drain on the spawn timer, as a fraction on top of real time. 0.5 means the timer runs at
    1.5x and arrivals come half again as fast. Ramped with the wave so the opening is still an
    opening, and capped so this never becomes its own runaway curve on top of the base one. */
 rate0:.55,
 ramp:.11,
 rateMax:1.6,
 /* V56's floor, raised. Five rather than three, reached later rather than sooner: five is at the
    limit of what a person tracks as individuals, and past it a crowd stops being a set of things
    and becomes a texture -- which is the mechanism by which density became noise the first time. */
 floorMax:5,
 floorRamp:.34
};

function v84Live(){return V84.on&&game.running&&!game.paused&&game.mode!=='duel';}

function v84Rate(){
 return Math.min(V84.rateMax,V84.rate0+Math.max(0,game.wave-1)*V84.ramp);
}

/* The floor, replaced rather than wrapped: V56 exposes it as a function on its own constant object,
   which is the seam it was written with. */
if(typeof V56==='object'){
 V56.floor=(wave)=>Math.min(V84.floorMax,1+Math.floor((wave-1)*V84.floorRamp));
}

const v84Update=update;
update=function(dt){
 v84Update(dt);
 if(!v84Live())return;
 /* The duel parks the timer at MAX_SAFE_INTEGER because its arrivals come from the server's
    schedule. Draining that would spawn a hostile the rival never sees. */
 if(game.spawnTimer===Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER)return;
 if(game.boss)return;
 game.spawnTimer=Math.max(0,game.spawnTimer-dt*v84Rate());
};
